A||||12||saleenfiero||Need opinion on use of parking brake||||4||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||04-28-2001||06:52 PM||||Okay, I need an opinion.  I've got an 86SE with 2.8 and slushbox.  Never use parking brake after the crap I put up with in my 85SE 2.5 (as in it would never release).  Anyways, I want to use it to keep my rear brakes adjusted, but should I run the risk of not being able to get the E-brake off.  I don't really have the time for downtime to get it fixed if it doesn't release.  So, should I try it, or no?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Mach10||04-28-2001||06:59 PM||||If you live on flat, level ground, you shouldn't normally need the e-brake. If you ever have to park on a slope, USE THE E-BRAKE unless you like bending/stretching the shift linkage, or damaging your parking pawl.<BR>I say go for it.<BR>Uh, dumb question, but you are familiar with the silly Fiero E-brake right? I wasn't, and thought it was broken  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||saleenfiero||04-28-2001||07:01 PM||||I think I know what you're talking about (e-brake light), how it is tempemental and only goes off at certain times, or are you referring to something else?<P>------------------<BR>'86 SE - My car is actually a MacLaren F1 with a Fiero body kit; or not.||||reg||1
Z||000003||shiloh187||04-28-2001||07:19 PM||||i think he is referring to how the e-brake on a fiero is made to be pulled up to engage, but then pushes back down while still engaged, to make getting in and out of the vehicle easier.<P>pull it up as far as you can (dont apply too much pull pressure) and push the release button in.  hold down the button and put it all the way back down.  that may make things easier on you hopefully. LMK.<P>shiloh<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||04-28-2001||07:22 PM||||Regular use of the Fiero hand brake keeps the rear brakes working right....<P>If your brakes haven't been used in a long time I'd take the wheels off and have a really good look at the cables and the levers. If they are corroded badly then you could have a problem. Cycle the handle with the wheels off so you can see what's up. If they don't  come off then you can pull them bake with big pliers in most cases. Worse case you cut the cables to release them.... ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Spektyr||04-28-2001||08:35 PM||||Is it just me or does it seem rather silly to put the parking brake on the wheels that are most likely to need jacked off the ground?<P>I mean, obviously you should block the opposite end's tires if you think there might be a chance of rolling when you jack a car up, but c'mon.  A front engine car has the front end off the ground probably twice as often as the back.  And they have rear parking brakes.  It would only make sense that a rear engine car (which will have the back end jacked more often) should have front parking brakes.<P>/rant off<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ed||04-28-2001||08:40 PM||||Expanding on Ogre's point:<P>The parking brake MUST be used on a regular basis to adjust the rear brakes.  I always use the parking brake, every time I park.  If yours has not been used, I also agree you should check it for corrosion.  Not using the brake will allow the internals of the brake wheel cylinder to corrode.  <P>Check your brakes, clean them up, and use the brake.   ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fropuff||04-28-2001||11:04 PM||||If it's stuck, all I do is use a hammer on the caliper to release it.  It works better than cutting it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||danny boy||04-28-2001||11:31 PM||||Of course, you can just eliminate the problem by removing the E brake all togather and use front calipers all the way around.  <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/PFF_sig_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||04-28-2001||11:37 PM||||Spektyr,<P>It is called an emregency brake for a reason. It not just a parking brake although that's how it's usually used.<P>It's on the rear because that's where it works best in most cases.<P>Yes, I do agree that Fiero is jacked in the back most often. But no, you really don't want this thing on the front.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Spektyr||04-29-2001||12:30 AM||||I'm familiar with the race application of the emergency brake (using it to produce gobs of oversteer).  However, given the location of the e-brake handle on the Fiero I would think it would be pretty hard to do that.<P>For 'emergency' situations, I'd think it would be safer to have the front locked up than the back.  Unless I'm mistaken virtually every car is set up with the braking bias towards the front.  Front tires locked means no steering, but no unexpected spins.<P>I'm sure I could be wrong...<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Master_Sushi||04-29-2001||12:02 PM||||I thought the parking brake was just for 1-80's <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">. tehehe||||reg||6
Z||000012||saleenfiero||04-29-2001||06:15 PM||||Okay, I just came in from seeing if my e-brake would seize on me or not.  It didn't, but the thing didn't holf the car at all either.  It ratcheted up to what seems like a normal level, but no holding power at all.  Does this mean it is totally inoperable, or should I still use it to maintain the rear pads?  Also, can anybody adjust this thing or is it better left to the pros?||||reg||1
